WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.120
Good to have you on the DP pod, the Dew Positive Podcast, episode 19 for July 25th, 2026.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:10.520
I'm Dan, and as always, Patrick's with me. Good news and science and tech today,

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:12.580
and one action that actually moves in number.

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:16.800
Morning. I spent the last hour reading the Yangtze numbers again,

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:18.780
and I keep landing on the same thing.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:21.080
The eat species are already showing bigger fish.

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.760
Exactly. After a full decade of no commercial fishing,

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:28.140
the biomass is responding faster than most models predicted.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:31.420
That feels like one of those stories where the mechanism is simple,

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:33.020
but the scale sneaks up on you.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.040
River recovery programs have been on the show before,

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.620
so the open question was always whether gains would stick once pressure dropped.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:42.860
Early data says they are.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:45.100
All right, time for the positive papers.

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:49.800
First up, the Yangtze fishing band, eight species have grown larger,

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:54.020
and ten more show population gains after 10 years of no commercial harvest.

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:58.480
The policy let natural reproduction run without constant removal,

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:02.980
and monitored stretches already record measurable biomass increases.

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:05.220
That fits the conservation arc we track.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:09.740
The open question was always which programs sustain gains once enforcement eases,

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:14.480
and this is the first clear signal that biomass rebounds before the full decade is even over.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:16.320
The mechanism is straightforward.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:21.200
Remove harvest pressure, and the existing adults get bigger and produce more offspring.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:24.720
What I like is that it happened without new technology,

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:26.420
just consistent policy.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:28.960
Still, enforcement across provinces could slip,

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:31.320
and climate stress on the river hasn't gone away,

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:36.220
so the next number I'll watch is whether those gains hold when the 10-year mark actually arrives.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:36.880
Fair.

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:41.860
Next, Northwell completed Phase 1 for DYP-688,

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:46.480
an antibody drug conjugate aimed at advanced UVL melanoma.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.320
The drug targets a specific tumor marker

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:53.660
and showed responses in the initial cohort while limiting systemic exposure.

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:56.560
This one lands in the global health progress we follow.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:00.780
The open question is which trial results actually reach deployed care,

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:05.040
and this is the first clinical signal for a targeted approach in a rare eye cancer

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:06.920
that has had almost no options.

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:11.340
The conjugate mechanism lets the payload activate mainly at the tumor site,

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:15.480
which is why off-target effects look lower than classic chemo.

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:19.960
Still early, though, most oncology candidates fail later stages.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:23.040
Exactly, safety data support moving to Phase 2,

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:26.980
but the real test is whether the responses scale in larger randomized trials.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:30.000
Northwell's team is already mapping dosing schedules.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:34.600
Last, a team led by SKKU Professor Hoseung Sao,

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:38.060
working with Wisconsin, Madison, and Washington researchers,

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:43.520
identified an atomic defect in zinc oxide that works as a stable spin qubit.

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:47.460
It holds coherence at higher temperatures than many alternatives,

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:49.480
which could reduce cooling needs.

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:51.700
That sits in the science frontier arc.

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:55.100
The open question is which lab results reach real deployment,

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:59.400
and this is still at materials characterization with no working processor yet.

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:02.960
The zinc oxide platform is already used in electronics,

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:06.600
so the integration path looks simpler than exotic materials.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:10.780
The coherence at warmer temperatures is the part that got me excited.

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:13.660
Hang on, higher temperature tolerance is elegant,

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:18.020
but the defect density still has to be controlled at scale for multi-qubit arrays.

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:20.740
That's the field failure mode I always flag.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:24.500
True. The collaboration measured the coherence properties,

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:28.420
but turning that into manufacturable chips is the next gate.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:29.900
There's your checklist again.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.220
Guilty time to think positive.

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:37.320
Tony Robbins points out that state drives behavior and that a clear decision

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:39.600
followed by massive action creates momentum.

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:42.840
When recovery data or early trial results land,

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.400
the instinct is to wait for perfect conditions.

00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:51.300
The mechanism he highlights is that small committed steps shift internal state faster than waiting does.

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:55.840
So the mental rep today is to name the next smallest action out loud

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:57.460
and take it within the hour.

00:03:57.900 --> 00:03:59.480
That matches the stories we just covered.

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:02.580
Instead of hoping the Yangtze gains continue on their own,

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:05.720
someone could schedule a call to a local conservation group,

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:10.940
same with the melanoma work, follow the phase two updates instead of assuming nothing will change.

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:16.420
It turns passive optimism into practiced agency without pretending mindset replaces

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:19.260
professional help when someone is genuinely struggling.

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:20.780
That brings us to the lover.

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:27.060
Install a certified plug-in solar panel on a balcony or yard and notify your utility first.

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:31.180
The unit connects to a standard outlet, costs a few hundred dollars,

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:34.540
and can offset several hundred kilowatt hours per year.

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:39.320
If 1% of urban households added one, the aggregate offset would reach hundreds of

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:44.420
gigawatt hours annually at the low end. Rough payback sits in the three to seven-year range,

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:46.340
depending on local rates and sunlight.

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:50.920
The first step is literally a five-minute call to confirm interconnection rules.

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:56.700
No roof work, no cruise. Households in sunny regions see faster offsets.

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:00.460
Northern climates still register measurable annual reductions.

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:05.860
I'm doing this one this week. I'll report back on the actual kilowatt hours once it's online.

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:10.660
Is this really the highest leverage hour for most listeners, though, or does it only win in certain

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:16.080
regions? The numbers are honest about regional variation. Still, the barrier is low enough

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:19.760
that the aggregate affects scales if adoption creeps up at all.

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:24.340
Okay, the payback math I'll give you. Now the do positive dispatch.

